3. Industry Case Study: Formula 1

In Formula 1, advanced data products are used to optimise performance in real time.

Challenges

Massive real-time data streams (car sensors, weather, track conditions)

Split-second performance optimisation

Enhancing fan engagement

Solutions

Integrated analytics systems combining telemetry, environmental, and track data

Machine learning models to optimise race strategy and car setup

Outcomes

Improved lap performance

Faster, data-driven decisions

Enhanced spectator engagement

Key Insight: Advanced data products create competitive advantage when real-time intelligence is embedded directly into operations.

4. Core Types of Advanced Data Products
1. Predictive Analytics

Uses historical data to forecast future outcomes.

Examples:

Retail demand forecasting

Patient risk prediction

Financial fraud detection

Value: Proactive rather than reactive decision-making.

2. Recommendation Systems

Analyse user behaviour to personalise suggestions.

Effectiveness depends on:

Behavioural data

Pattern recognition

Continuous learning

Value: Increased engagement, conversion, and customer satisfaction.

3. Decision Support Systems (DSS)

Support complex business decisions by combining data, analytics, and business rules.

They:

Improve efficiency

Enhance strategic decisions

Reduce uncertainty

They support human judgment — they do not replace it.

4. APIs & Microservices

Enable systems to communicate and scale efficiently.

For example, the Google Maps API allows businesses to integrate mapping and geolocation into applications.

Why they matter:

Seamless integration

Scalability

Real-time data exchange

Modular architecture

5. Knowledge Check – Key Takeaways

Predictive analytics applies across retail, healthcare, finance — and more.

Recommendation systems rely on behavioural analysis.

Decision support systems enhance — not eliminate — human decision-making.

APIs and microservices enable integration across systems.

